body:
### Tested versions

Tested in 4.4.1 stable, 4.5 beta7

### System information

Windows 11 - Vulkan 1.4.315 - Forward+

### Issue description

I tried using compute shader to calculate SDF images. First, use composer effect and Camera3D in a SubViewport to calculate a SDF viewport texture. And then send it to the main camera's composer effect to perform more calculations.

But I found that the values in the original SDF image were mapped to other values. For example, 0.5 has become around 0.7. It is speculated that the SDF image was converted from linear to sRGB.

But when I searched for viewport documents, I did not find any relevant descriptions. Also i have read some issues like #84989, but this one seems probably not related to HDR 2D. And it may be more relevant to #9957, but there is no transparency here.

**I want to know if this behavior is expected? And how to control this behavior in the compositor effect?**

### Steps to reproduce

I will reproduce it using another simple example:

1. Create a scene with **Camera3D A** and a SubViewport.
2. Add another Camera3D B into SubViewport, and write a compositor effect to generate color for **viewport texture**.
3. In **Camera3D A**, read that **viewport texture** via sampler2D and check it value.

<img width="1024" height="1024" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7a959c47-99cf-4ac7-9a3a-298ee31084e3" />

You can notice the color in the subviewport (top-right) is linear, but after reading it, it becomes sRGB.

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

See: [test.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/22175128/test.zip)

If you uncomment the code in `reader.glsl`, two color will become same.

```glsl
#[compute]
#version 450

layout(local_size_x = 16, local_size_y = 16, local_size_z = 1) in;
layout(set = 0, binding = 0, rgba16f) uniform restrict writeonly image2D output_texture;
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) uniform sampler2D input_texture;


float srgb_to_linear(float x)
{
    return x < 0.04045
        ? x * 0.0773993808
        : pow(x * 0.9478672986 + 0.0521327014, 2.4);
}


void main()
{
    ivec2 size = imageSize(output_texture);
    ivec2 pixel = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
    vec2 uv = vec2(pixel) / vec2(size);
    vec2 value = texture(input_texture, uv).xy;
    // value.x = srgb_to_linear(value.x);
    // value.y = srgb_to_linear(value.y);
    imageStore(output_texture, pixel, vec4(value, 1.0, 1.0));
}
```
